To write a C++ function that takes a reference to a `std::string` containing markdown formatted text and returns a `std::string` containing HTML formatted text, you can use a library like `markdowncpp` or `libmarkdown`. Here's an example using `markdowncpp`:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
#include <markdown/markdown.h>

std::string markdownToHtml(const std::string& markdownText) {
    markdown::Markdown md;
    std::string htmlText = md.render(markdownText);
    return htmlText;
}

int main() {
    std::string markdownText = "# Markdown Text\n\nThis is a sample text.";
    std::string htmlText = markdownToHtml(markdownText);
    std::cout << htmlText << std::endl;
    return 0;
}
```

This function uses `markdowncpp` to convert the markdown text to HTML. The `markdown::Markdown` class is used to render the markdown text and return the resulting HTML string. The `main` function demonstrates how to use the `markdownToHtml` function.

To install `markdowncpp`, you can use a package manager like `cpkg` or download it from the GitHub repository. For `libmarkdown`, you can use a package manager like `pip` or download it from the official website.

